rouped under \"Due today\" or \"Due this week\" etc.\n\nBut what's the correct grouping? \"Due date grouping: boundaries between groups computed with previous comparison logic.\" In tasks.org, group headers: \"Due today\", \"Due tomorrow\", \"Due this week\", \"Due next week\", \"Due <date>\"? Hmm. Actually tasks.org groups by due date (individual date), so each distinct due date is a group with header like \"Due Thursday\" / \"Due Friday, December 4\". Wait, here grouping shows each distinct date as its own group header. \"Due Thursday\" (Nov 19), \"Due Tuesday, November 24\", \"Due Friday, December 4\", \"No due date\". So grouping by due date means distinct dates each group. So what are \"boundaries between groups\"? The comparison logic determines whether a header displays relative weekday (\"Due Thursday\") or full date (\"Due Tuesday, November 24\"), based on distance from today. That's the \"previous comparison logic\" — the boundary between relative and full date formats! E.g., if within 6 days show weekday only, else show full date. Buy milk Nov 19 = 5 days → \"Due Thursday\"; Pack Nov 24 = 10 days → full. Hmm but what about Nov 20/21? Boundary between \"next week\" and \"later\"? In tasks.org, header format likely: dates within the current week (up to Saturday? or within 6 days?) show weekday only.\n\nHmm, the changed note says \"the boundaries between groups are now computed with the previous comparison logic.\" This is ambiguous. Could be hidden bug about date group formatting boundary. Let's test boundaries: tasks due tomorrow (Nov 15 Sunday), due Nov 21 (next Saturday, 7 days), due Nov 22 (8 days). Header display rules? In stock tasks.org, I believe the group header for a date within the next 6 days shows weekday name; beyond that shows full date \"Tuesday, November 24\". Hmm, but then Nov 20 (Friday, 6 days) shows \"Due Friday\" and Nov 21 (Sat, 7 days) shows \"Due Saturday, November 21\". Let's test with Read docs: set due date today/tomorrow/Nov 21 