{"type": "thinking", "text": "Base state restored except Show full date ON. Also, Buy milk due date now is Nov 19 (same). Good.\n\nNow let's test due date grouping boundaries. Plan: use Read docs (currently no due date) as a test subject: set due date to Today → observe group header; then tomorrow; then maybe Nov 15.. Actually group header for a task due today: \"Due today\" likely. Let's test each of days 0,1,2,...,8 maybe. But that's many edits. Perhaps just key boundaries: today, tomorrow, and the \"within a week vs beyond\" boundary. The grouping code likely labels: \"Due today\", \"Due tomorrow\", then weekday for dates within the next 6 days? and full date after. Hmm.\n\nWait, but existing Buy milk (5 days) shows \"Due Thursday\", and Nov 24 (10 days) shows full. Need to find where boundary is: maybe header uses weekday within 6 days (up to +6) and full date at +7? Or within the \"current week\" (until Sunday)? Hmm.\n\nLet me think about tasks.org source: The task list header formatting: for grouping by due date, group header uses `DateStrings.getRelativeDateString(context, date)`? Something like: If date is today → \"Due today\"; tomorrow → \"Due tomorrow\"; within next 6 days → \"Due Thursday\"; else → \"Due Thursday, November 19\" full. Hmm, I recall group headers in Tasks.org: \"Due today\", \"Due tomorrow\", \"Due this week\"?? No, grouping by due date is per-date, and each header shows e.g. \"Due Monday\", \"Due Tuesday, November 24\". Hmm.\n\nActually, I now recall: Tasks.org's date headers show \"Due today\", \"Due tomorrow\", \"Due Thursday\", \"Due Nov 19\"? Not sure. Let me just test with today and tomorrow and see.\n\nUse Read docs: edit, set due today, save, observe header. Then change to tomorrow, etc. Let's do a sequence: Today → header? Then tomorrow (Nov 15) → header? Then Nov 20 (Fri, +6) → header \"Due Friday\"? Then Nov 21 (Sat, +7) → header? Then Nov 22 (Sun, +8)? Then revert to no due date.\n\nActually editing Read docs repeatedly is fine but time-consuming. Each edit: open task, tap due row, tap date, OK, Save, observe. ~6 steps per date. 4 dates = 24 steps. Acceptable.\n\nBut wait, is grouping per-date or per-boundary? The header for Nov 24 is \"Due Tuesday, November 24\" while Nov 19 \"Due Thursday\". Since both have same formatting except short vs full, maybe the grouping is by due date and labels are relative. The \"boundaries between groups\" phrase suggests grouping into buckets like \"Overdue\", \"Today\", \"Tomorrow\", \"This week\", \"Next week\", \"Later\". But we only see distinct dates as headers... Hmm, unless each group is by exact date, and \"boundaries\" refers to bucket intervals? No.\n\nWait, maybe the grouping is not by exact date but by calendar bucket? Let's re-examine: Buy milk due Nov 19 → header \"Due Thursday\". Pack for trip due Nov 24 → \"Due Tuesday, November 24\". Pay rent Dec 4 → \"Due Friday, December 4\". If grouping were \"This week\" vs \"Next week\", headers would say \"Due this week\". They don't.\n\nIn tasks.org v14, I believe group headers display the date itself, with relative wording. So \"boundaries\" likely refers to the threshold between relative and full formats. The changed note \"the boundaries between groups are now computed with the previous comparison logic\" might mean: previous logic used `date < today + 7` while new logic uses something off-by-one. Testing Nov 20 vs Nov 21 vs Nov 22 could reveal.\n\nWhat is expected format? In tasks.org, the date header format: I recall in the app, headers look like \"Due today\", \"Due tomorrow\", \"Due Friday\", \"Due Nov 20\". Hmm, if within 6 days → weekday; else → \"MMM d\" maybe. But here Nov 24 shows \"Tuesday, November 24\" full. So full format = \"EEEE, MMMM d\". Relative = \"EEEE\". Boundary unknown. If the format function is \"within 6 days → weekday only, else full\", then Nov 19 (5 days) weekday, Nov 20 (6 days) weekday, Nov 21 (7 days) full. Let's test Nov 21 and Nov 20.\n\nAlternatively the boundary might be \"within the current week\" i.e., dates until end of week (Sat Nov 14 is today, so only today)! But Buy milk Nov 19 (next week) shows \"Due Thursday\" — so not week-based.\n\nSo boundary likely day-count based. Test +6 (Nov 20) and +7 (Nov 21). If +6 shows full date, then boundary is +5 (i.e., previous comparison logic uses `< today+6`?), likely bug. Which is \"correct\"? Hmm, hard without ground truth. Typical expected: within 6 days? Hmm.\n\nHonestly, the changed note \"boundaries between groups are now computed with the previous comparison logic\" might be a deliberate hint of a specific bug: e.g., using a comparison that includes the boundary wrongly: `date <= today + 6` vs `< today + 7` — same. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the \"previous comparison logic\" refers to comparing Date objects by time rather than by day, causing a task due \"today\" with time later than now to be grouped as \"tomorrow\"? Hmm, that's more like \"comparison logic\". Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, the grouping boundaries might be about the \"completed tasks\" section or \"overdue\" boundary: overdue tasks (due before today) vs today. Since our data has no overdue tasks, we can test by setting a due date in the past (e.g., yesterday Nov 13) and see header \"Due yesterday\" or \"Overdue\"? In tasks.org, overdue tasks get grouped under \"Overdue\" perhaps? Hmm.\n\nLet's test a past date: Read docs due yesterday → header? If the comparison boundary is broken, e.g., a task due today appears under \"Overdue\" or a task due yesterday appears under \"Due today\". That's a strong candidate for \"boundaries between groups computed with previous comparison logic\". Since all current tasks are future, maybe the bug only manifests today/past boundaries.\n\nLet me test with Read docs:\n1. due today (Nov 14) → expect \"Due today\" (maybe \"Due Today\").\n2. due yesterday (Nov 13) → expect \"Overdue\" or \"Due Friday\"?\n3. due tomorrow (Nov 15) → \"Due tomorrow\".\n\nLet's start with due today. Open Read docs."}
