{"type": "thinking", "text": "Buy milk row shows only \"Nov 19\" due badge. No start date badge despite start date set to Nov 14. Hmm. Perhaps start-date badge only shows when \"Start date\" chip enabled — it is enabled. So likely bug #2: start date badge missing. But wait: maybe the start date chip isn't shown because start date is today (in the past/today) and it's only shown in \"Start date\" grouping? Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe the start date chip only displays for tasks with times? Hmm. Let me check by opening Buy milk editor to confirm start date is there. Then maybe change start date to Nov 16 (future) and see. Actually, the \"start date\" in tasks.org is like \"start working on this date\" and maybe chips show start date only when start date is in the future? It is today = not future, so maybe hidden? Unclear.\n\nThe changed note: \"Date badges on task rows: the badge for each date field is now built independently of the others.\" This suggests a bug where e.g. the start date badge is built from the due date (showing same date) or vice versa, or one is missing when both present. We have both: start Nov 14, due Nov 19, only due badge shows. That looks like the start badge is missing. Hmm, but maybe by design start date chip only appears as a chip after the title? In tasks.org, start date appears as a badge just like due date? Hmm.\n\nLet me check tasks.org knowledge: In the task list, the row shows due date on the right, and start date maybe as a small icon/left? Actually the \"chips\" settings (Subtasks, Start date, Places, Lists, Tags) refer to chips shown under the title (the \"2\" subtask count chip is shown under Pack for trip title!). Indeed, the \"2\" chip with chevron under Pack for trip is the Subtasks chip. So \"Start date\" chip would appear under the title as a chip, not the right-side badge. Hmm! So the right-side due date is the \"date badge\", and the chips are these little rounded items: Subtasks count, start date, places, lists, tags. So the start date chip should appear under the title as a chip like \"Nov 14\" perhaps. Not shown either. Hmm.\n\nWait, the note says \"Date badges on task rows: the badge for each date field is now built independently of the others.\" So date badges might be those chips for start date and due date? Hmm. But due date appears on the right, not a chip.\n\nIn tasks.org, I recall:\n- Row right side: due date badge (e.g., \"Nov 19\") in gray/red.\n- Below title: chips for subtask count, start date? Hmm.\n\nActually maybe in newer tasks.org, chips appear at the right? Hmm.\n\nLet me verify the Start date chip by grouping by start date perhaps. But the chip should show regardless.\n\nLet me test: change Buy milk's start date to Nov 16 (future) and see if a chip appears. If still nothing, then start date chip is broken. But also maybe start date chip appears only when start date is \"hidden\"? Let me just test quickly: edit Buy milk start date to Nov 16, save. Observe. Then revert.\n\nActually, hold on. Maybe the chips are not enabled because \"Show description\" is on and the chip row is on second line — description \"2 litres\" occupies it. For Pack for trip, the \"2\" chip appears on second line because there's no description. For Buy milk, description occupies second line; maybe chips should still appear after description on same or third line? Hmm.\n\nIn tasks.org, the row layout: title line; second line: description (truncated) then chips? I think chips appear to the right of description on the same line? Hmm. For Buy milk, description \"2 litres\" is short; there's space to its right, but no start chip. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the \"Start date\" chip only shows for start dates in the future relative to... hmm.\n\nLet me check the \"Subtasks\" chip for Pack: \"2\" with up-chevron (expand). That's the subtask count chip. Good. So chips exist.\n\nNow let me look at the Start date chip behavior by setting start date Nov 16. Let me do it.\n\nActually, also reconsider: maybe the Start date chip appears only if \"Start date\" != due date and \"start date is in the future\"? Let me just test."}
