{"type": "thinking", "text": "Now Buy milk row has a start date chip: \"pending_actions | Mon\" — a chip showing \"Mon\" with an icon. So start date badge (chip) shows when start date is Nov 16 (future), but not when start date = today (Nov 14). Hmm, maybe by design: start date chip not shown when start date is today or past? Hmm, wait, earlier with start date today it didn't show. Interesting. Maybe the chip only shows when start date > today? Or maybe the chip for start date today was omitted because \"Start date\" is in the past? Unclear.\n\nActually, maybe start date chip uses the \"start date\" and is hidden when start date <= today? Tasks.org might not show start date chips for started tasks? Possibly. Hmm.\n\nBut interesting: the chip shows \"Mon\" while the due badge shows \"Nov 19\". Note the format inconsistency: start date chip \"Mon\" vs due date badge \"Nov 19\". Hmm, is that the bug? \"badge for each date field is now built independently of the others\" — the badge builder for each date field computes relative/absolute formatting independently. Maybe expected start date chip shows \"Nov 16\" or \"Mon\"? Hmm.\n\nWait, actually the format difference could be intentional: start date chips show relative weekday? Hmm.\n\nHmm, \"Mon\" for Nov 16 (2 days away) — due badge for Nov 19 shows \"Nov 19\" not \"Thu\". That's inconsistent relative formatting between the two date fields! Maybe that's the bug: the badges for start and due date use different date formats. In stock tasks.org, both badges likely use the same format (e.g., \"Mon\" for dates within the week, \"Nov 24\" beyond). Our due badge \"Nov 19\" for a date 5 days away shows absolute, while start date \"Mon\" (Nov 16, 2 days) shows relative. Hmm, but that could be because they're computed independently... The changed note says \"the badge for each date field is now built independently of the others\" — the bug might be that the due badge uses a different comparison/boundary than the start badge. E.g., start chip \"Mon\" (relative), due badge \"Nov 19\" (absolute). In stock, due badge would show \"Thu\" for Nov 19? Hmm.\n\nHold on — maybe the format depends on \"Show full date\" setting: Show full date OFF originally; now it's ON. Both badges now: \"Mon\" and \"Nov 19\". Hmm.\n\nActually maybe the badge format is: date within 7 days → weekday (e.g., \"Mon\"), beyond → \"Nov 19\". Nov 16 = Monday, 2 days away → \"Mon\". Nov 19 = Thursday, 5 days away → should be \"Thu\" but shows \"Nov 19\". That's inconsistent! Unless the due date badge uses a different boundary (e.g., only within 3 days?). Nov 16 shows \"Mon\", Nov 19 shows \"Nov 19\". The difference could be due to \"Show full date\" affecting due badge only? No.\n\nHmm, but wait: earlier with Show full date OFF, overdue badge \"Nov 13\" showed \"Nov 13\" (not \"Yesterday\" or \"Fri\"). So due overdue badge absolute. Hmm.\n\nLet me gather more data: Read docs due Nov 13 shows \"Nov 13\" (badge). Buy milk due Nov 19 shows \"Nov 19\". Pay rent Dec 4 \"Dec 4\". Pack Nov 24 \"Nov 24\". Start date Nov 16 shows \"Mon\". So due badge always absolute date MMM d; start chip shows weekday for within 6 days. In stock tasks.org, I believe the start date chip and due date badge might indeed have different formats: due date badge is at right and shows date; start date chip shows relative weekday? Hmm.\n\nHonestly, I need better ground truth about Tasks.org behavior. Let me think harder about tasks.org UI (version 14.x, 2025):\n\nTask row layout:\n[checkbox] Title                         [due date]\n           description\n           [chips: subtask count, start date, list, tags]\n\nI believe the right-side due date is shown as e.g. \"Today\", \"Tomorrow\", \"Nov 19\", or \"Nov 19\" always? In Tasks.org I remember due dates on the right in bold colored text: for today it says \"Today\", overdue \"Yesterday\", otherwise \"Nov 19\" style. Yes! I'm fairly confident: the due date badge shows \"Today\"/\"Tomorrow\"/weekday? Hmm.\n\nLet me test: set Read docs due today (currently Nov 13 overdue) and check badge text. If it shows \"Today\", then the badge format uses relative labels for today/tomorrow; maybe weekday for within a week? We saw overdue shows \"Nov 13\" not \"Yesterday\", so maybe not relative for past. But maybe \"Yesterday\" is not used.\n\nHmm, this is a fuzzy area. The build changed explicitly: \"Date badges on task rows: the badge for each date field is now built independently of the others.\" So there is a bug in badge building. Since all due badges show absolute \"Nov 19\" even for nearby dates while start chip shows relative \"Mon\", likely the due badge lost its relative formatting (or start date badge has extra). Which is correct? Need to determine expected behavior.\n\nThink about tasks.org implementation: There's `DateStrings` with `getRelativeDateString` used for date chips. In the task list item, there's a `due_date` TextView showing `getRelativeDateString(dueDate)`. This function returns \"Today\", \"Tomorrow\", or e.g. \"Nov 19\"? Hmm.\n\nActually, I recall that in tasks.org, the due date badge in the list shows e.g. \"Nov 19\" — absolute date, in colored text. Hmm, wait, no. Let me visualize a screenshot of Tasks.org: Rows look like:\n\n☐ Buy milk                                   Today\n   2 litres\n\nHmm, I'm not certain.\n\nAlternatively, the badge may be formatted as \"Mon, Nov 16\" etc.\n\nLet me search memory: Tasks.org (Android) task list item with due date shows right-aligned text like \"May 21\" in a colored chip? I recall the due date displayed as a small gray text \"Mon\" or red \"Yesterday\"? Hmm. In Tasks.org's default theme, the date text on the right is red for overdue, black/gray for future. I remember screenshots: \"Today\", \"Tomorrow\" maybe.\n\nHmm. The changed note says \"badge for each date field is now built independently of the others\". This implies previously one badge string was built from both dates (maybe concatenated), now each built separately. A bug could be: start date badge incorrectly shows due date? Our data: start chip \"Mon\" = Nov 16 = start date correct. Due badge \"Nov 19\" correct. So both correct values, just format difference. Hmm.\n\nBut maybe \"Mon\" is wrong: the start date chip should show \"Nov 16\" (absolute) like due badge. Or maybe due badge should show \"Thu\". Which one is off? Given consistent formatting is desirable, it's a plausible report: date badge formatting differs between start and due date fields (start shows weekday \"Mon\", due shows \"Nov 19\") though both are within the same week. Hmm, but I need to be careful not to report a non-bug.\n\nLet me get more evidence by testing with \"Show full date\" toggled. Currently ON. Turn it OFF and see badges: start chip and due badge. If both change consistently, then the formats are tied to setting. Let's turn Show full date OFF (revert to original too). Then check.\n\nActually \"Show full date\" ON should show full date everywhere, e.g., \"Monday, November 16\" — but chip shows \"Mon\". Wait maybe with Show full date ON, the formatted dates should be full. The editor showed full dates. Group headers still \"Due Thursday\". Hmm, so Show full date affects editor rows only? Weird.\n\nLet me toggle OFF and observe badges. Go to settings. This is getting long but useful.\n\nActually, maybe I should also check the list with grouping by due date again, since due badges hidden when grouped by due date is maybe by design. Let me hold.\n\nPriority: find actual bugs. So far candidates:\nA) Date badges start/due formatting inconsistency (uncertain).\nB) Due date badge hidden when grouped by due date (likely by design, since header shows date).\nC) Grouping header \"Overdue\" works.\nD) Start date chip missing for start date = today (might be by design? tasks starting today... hmm).\n\nHmm wait, maybe the start date chip is missing when start date <= today because the chip may only show future start dates? Let's test start date yesterday? Not needed.\n\nLet me check the due badge format when task due tomorrow (Nov 15) with grouping None. Read docs due today→tomorrow. The badge for Nov 15 would reveal relative formatting (\"Tomorrow\"). Then due Nov 16 → \"Mon\"? If due Nov 16 shows \"Nov 16\" while start Nov 16 shows \"Mon\", that's a clear inconsistency bug.\n\nLet me do Read docs due Nov 16 then check badge. But careful: current Read docs due Nov 13 (o", "truncated": true}
