{"type": "thinking", "text": "Confirmed: Read docs due Nov 16 shows due badge \"Nov 16\". Buy milk start date Nov 16 shows chip \"Mon\". So the same date is displayed differently: due badge \"Nov 16\" vs start chip \"Mon\". Is this a bug? The changed note: \"Date badges on task rows: the badge for each date field is now built independently of the others.\" If the intended behavior is each date field badge built independently (same formatting logic), then having start=\"Mon\" and due=\"Nov 16\" is inconsistent — one of them likely wrong. But which is expected by tasks.org? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe in tasks.org, the start date chip legitimately shows relative weekday, while due date badge shows date. Let me recall... Actually, I believe in Tasks.org, the due date badge on the right shows text like \"Nov 16\" (absolute), and start date chip shows \"Mon\" (relative). Hmm, I honestly can't recall.\n\nHmm, let's consider the \"date badges\" wording: \"the badge for each date field is now built independently of the others.\" There are multiple date fields: start date, due date, maybe \"hide until\"/reminder. The bug (likely) is in the new independent-building code: e.g., the start date badge incorrectly picks the due date string, or the due badge not built. Our observation: start chip \"Mon\" correct date (Nov 16), due badge \"Nov 16\" correct date. Values correct. Format differs but maybe by design.\n\nWait, maybe the start chip has an icon (pending_actions) and shows relative \"Mon\", while due badge shows \"Nov 16\". That could be exactly stock behavior: start dates show \"Mon\" (relative) because chip has a \"start\" icon and date string `getRelativeDateString`. Hmm.\n\nLet me look for a more definite bug with date badges: \"built independently of the others\" suggests previously the badge text was shared, e.g., a single variable used for both start & due badge. A bug might be: on a row with both start and due dates, the start chip and due badge values get mixed up (e.g., due badge shows start date). Our data shows distinct correct values. So maybe fine.\n\nHmm. Let me not invent a bug. Let me focus on clear bug candidates.\n\nActually, wait! Look at the sort with grouping None: rows sorted by due date ascending. But \"Read docs\" due Nov 16 is above \"Buy milk\" due Nov 19 — correct. However, note \"Buy milk\" has start Nov 16 and due Nov 19.\n\nNow, what about the \"Overdue\" group header? With grouping by due date, Read docs overdue Nov 13 got \"Overdue\". Good.\n\nLet me look at the grouping boundary issue more carefully. The item \"Due date grouping in the task list: the boundaries between groups are now computed with the previous comparison logic.\" Hmm — maybe this refers to the relative labels on group headers (e.g., \"Due today\", \"Due tomorrow\", \"Due Thursday\", full date) using the \"previous\" comparison logic (maybe off-by-one). We should test boundaries: due dates +1 to +8 and see labels. The expected labels? Let's reason from the app's date shortcut semantics: due picker shortcuts \"Today\", \"Tomorrow\", \"Next Sat\". \"Next Sat\" = Nov 21 (a week from today since today is Sat). Hmm.\n\nIn Tasks.org, group header format is likely via `DateStrings.getRelativeDateString`: which returns \"Today\"/\"Tomorrow\"/\"Yesterday\" or \"EEEE\" within 6 days or \"EEEE, MMMM d\" beyond. We observed:\n- Nov 13 (-1) → \"Overdue\" (separate overdue bucket)\n- Nov 14 (0) → \"Due today\"\n- Nov 19 (+5) → \"Due Thursday\"\n- Nov 24 (+10) → \"Due Tuesday, November 24\"\n- Dec 4 (+20) → \"Due Friday, December 4\"\n\nWe need +1, +6, +7. Expected: Nov 15 → \"Due tomorrow\" probably. Nov 21 (+7): if relative window is 6 days, full date \"Due Saturday, November 21\". Nov 20 (+6) → \"Due Friday\". If the comparison logic is off by one, maybe +6 shows full date or +7 shows weekday. Which is \"previous comparison logic\"? Hmm.\n\nLet me test with Read docs (currently due Nov 16). I'll use it to test Nov 20 (+6) and Nov 21 (+7) and Nov 15 (+1) and Nov 17 (+3) maybe. Grouping currently \"None\" — I need grouping by due date to see headers. Sort sheet → grouping → By due date. Then set dates.\n\nActually, let's restore grouping to \"By due date\" now (original setting). Then test headers.\n\nSort sheet [22]."}
