{"type": "thinking", "text": "Base state restored (start date removed, Show full date off, Morning 9AM, grouping by due date, sorting by due date ascending, Read docs no due date, Pay rent repeats monthly from due date presumably). Good.\n\nNow let's explore other aspects for bugs: task list row display, task editor date settings, subtasks/repeating. Potential bugs might be more obvious than I think. Let me reconsider the \"date badges\" changed item: maybe the bug is that with grouping by due date, due-date badges disappear — but stock behavior?? Let me verify by checking whether the date badge being hidden in grouped view is expected. In Tasks.org, I'm fairly sure when grouped by due date, the right-side due date badge is indeed hidden because redundant. Hmm, but actually I recall seeing both the group header AND right-side date in Tasks.org? Let me think of tasks.org screenshots... The default list view: group headers \"Today\", \"Tomorrow\", then rows; I don't recall date badges on rows because grouping by due date is default; and indeed tasks.org's default might hide due date chips when grouped by due date. Yes, that's likely intended.\n\nHmm. Let me check the \"Chips\" settings: Due date is not a chip; start date is. The due date badge is separate. OK.\n\nSo where's the date badge bug? \"badge for each date field is now built independently of the others\" — the start date chip and due date badge are both \"date badges\"? Maybe the bug is that the start date chip shows due date formatting \"Mon\" vs due badge \"Nov 16\". Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe there's another date field: \"reminder\"/\"hide until\"? No.\n\nHold on: maybe the due date badge (right side) is built using the *first* date field: due date. The start date chip shows \"Mon\". If the code change made badges built independently, maybe there's an issue where a task with a start date but no due date shows the start date in the due badge slot too, duplicating? Let's test: create a task with only start date (no due date) and check the row. In grouped by due date view, it would go under \"No due date\" — with a start chip. Maybe the due badge incorrectly displays the start date. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, test a task with both start and due dates, check that the due badge uses due date (it did: \"Nov 19\") and start chip uses start date (\"Mon\"). Values correct. So no mixing bug.\n\nMaybe the bug is about formatting: chip shows \"Mon\" while badge shows \"Nov 19\" — the expected per tasks.org: due badge shows due date in \"MMM d\" always; start chip shows start date in relative weekday if within 6 days? Hmm, maybe stock does exactly that: start date chip uses relative format; due badge uses absolute. Not sure.\n\nLet me check tasks.org source knowledge: There is `DateStrings.getRelativeDateString` used for chips? I remember tasks.org task list shows date chips with `getRelativeDateString` for both start and due dates, e.g., a task due today shows \"Today\" in red on the right. Hmm! Actually yes, I think the due date on the right shows \"Today\" when due today, and \"Tomorrow\" when due tomorrow. For future dates > tomorrow maybe \"Nov 19\"? Hmm.\n\nLet me test that: with grouping None, set a task due today and check badge; due tomorrow → check badge. Let's do with Read docs (temp). This is quick-ish and would clarify format rules. If due today shows \"Today\" and due tomorrow \"Tomorrow\", then badges use relative strings for today/tomorrow and absolute otherwise (likely consistent). Then the start chip \"Mon\" for Nov 16 is inconsistent with \"Nov 16\" due badge — suggests start chip uses weekday format. Hmm.\n\nActually wait: the start chip \"Mon\" — the chip includes a \"pending_actions\" icon and \"Mon\" text. Maybe the start date chip shows \"Mon\" because start dates are displayed as weekday to save space? Could be stock. Ugh.\n\nLet me look at the app's widget \"Chip appearance\" setting: \"Text and icon\" means chips show icon+text. The start date chip shows icon (pending_actions is the icon ligature name) + \"Mon\". So the chip text is \"Mon\" — maybe stock indeed uses `getRelativeDateString` for start dates, which gives weekday for within a week.\n\nHmm. But the due date badge for Nov 16 showed \"Nov 16\", not \"Mon\". If both used the same `getRelativeDateString`, they'd both be \"Mon\". The changed note says each badge built independently — maybe the bug is the due date badge isn't using the relative formatter? But maybe due date badge always absolute by design.\n\nI need a reference. Let me think about tasks.org screenshots I've seen: In Tasks.org list, the right side due date shows e.g. \"Nov 19\" in small text? I genuinely recall seeing dates like \"Apr 30\" on the right in Tasks.org, colored red when overdue. Hmm, and for today maybe it shows \"Today\". Not sure.\n\nHmm, maybe I can find evidence in the app itself: the \"Show full date\" preference! What does it do? Maybe it toggles the due badge between relative short (\"Mon\") and full (\"Monday, November 16\")? We toggled ON and the due badge still showed \"Nov 19\"; hmm no. Wait, with Show full date ON, group headers remained \"Due Thursday\" and due badge \"Nov 19\". So maybe the setting only affects editor date rows (which showed full dates). Weird setting name.\n\nHmm. Let me test \"Show full date\" again specifically with grouping None to observe due badges & start chips under ON vs OFF. Maybe with ON, badges change to \"Monday, November 16\"? Earlier screenshot with ON showed \"Nov 19\" — no.\n\nOK. Let me try a different approach: inspect the app version and maybe known behavior via the \"About\" or changelog? Not helpful.\n\nPerhaps I'm overanalyzing. Maybe the injected bug for date badges is different: \"the badge for each date field is now built independently of the others\" — think of a task with both start and due date. Before: a single builder built a combined badge (\"Nov 16 – Nov 19\")? Now: independent. A bug could be that the due date badge is hidden 