er uses ≤6 and the picker uses ≤7, that's an inconsistency between formats, but is it a bug? The changed note says grouping boundaries use \"previous comparison logic\" — maybe expected to use the same DateStrings function as the date picker, and the bug is that group headers treat +7 as full date while date picker (and perhaps start chip) treat +7 as weekday. Hmm! That could be the injected bug: boundaries differ by one day. But which is \"correct\"? The \"previous comparison logic\" being old suggests the new build should use old logic; the boundary might be off by one compared to the rest of the app (date picker). Hmm, but we don't know old logic.\n\nTest: sort/group with due Nov 22 and see header full; the date picker for Nov 22 would show \"Sunday, November 22\" presumably. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, let me test the start chip for Nov 22: start chip would show \"Sun\" if within 6, \"Nov 22\" if 7+? Earlier start chip for Nov 20? We didn't test. The start chip for Nov 16 showed \"Mon\" (within 6). Due badge always absolute.\n\nHmm, what function does the start chip use? Maybe `DateStrings.getRelativeDateString` with a 7-day window? Let's test start chip for Nov 21 (+7): if it shows \"Sat\" then start chip uses ≤7 window while group header uses ≤6. That would indicate a boundary inconsistency introduced. Hmm, but it's not proof of a bug; different widgets can use different formats by design.\n\nUgh, this is hard without ground truth. Let me look for more decisive bugs.\n\nMaybe the \"previous comparison logic\" refers to sorting: grouping by due date boundaries might include tasks due today in \"Overdue\" if their time has passed — we tested today 9 AM → Due today. Hmm fine.\n\nWhat about tasks with no due date but with a start date? Grouping by due date puts them in \"No due date\"? Probably.\n\nWhat about overdue tasks with grouping by due date: We saw \"Overdue\" header. In stock tasks.org, I think overdue tasks are grouped under \"Overdue\" only if \"Show overdue