{"type": "thinking", "text": "Interesting: the editor start date row now shows \"Tomorrow\" instead of a date (\"Sunday, November 15\"). So the start date set via the calendar shortcut \"Today/Tomorrow\" gets displayed as relative \"Tomorrow\"! That suggests the app persists relative start dates when chosen via relative shortcuts. Wait, I tapped the calendar day Nov 15 (not the \"Tomorrow\" shortcut; the start picker's shortcut list initially didn't have Tomorrow). Hmm, but after tapping calendar Nov 15, the top row became \"Tomorrow\". The editor row shows \"Tomorrow\". So selecting the calendar day that equals tomorrow results in the start date being stored/displayed as \"Tomorrow\" — i.e., relative label. Hmm, maybe the app intentionally displays relative labels in the editor for start dates (\"Tomorrow\", \"Day before due\", \"Monday\"?). Earlier start date Nov 16 displayed as \"Monday\", not \"Monday, November 16\". Start date Nov 14 displayed as \"Today\"? Actually the day I set start=Nov 14, the editor showed \"Today\" (element 3 \"Today\") — yes! And Nov 16 showed \"Monday\". So start date editor row always shows a relative label (\"Today\"/\"Tomorrow\"/\"Monday\"), not a full date. With Show full date ON, it showed \"Saturday, November 14\" (full). So the \"Show full date\" setting controls whether editor shows relative short or full. That's a coherent design: relative short labels by default.\n\nSo \"Day before due\" for relative start date is also consistent — it's a relative start date. OK, not a bug.\n\nNow save this and check row in \"No due date\" group: does the row show a start chip and no due badge (due badge slot empty)? Save."}
