{"type": "thinking", "text": "Wait, element 3 start date row now shows \"Due date\"?! I thought start date was \"Tomorrow\". Hmm: start date row text is \"Due date\"?? That means the start date is set to a relative value \"Due date\" (i.e., start = due date). When I set start date to Nov 15 (which equaled the due date Nov 15? No, due date was none at that time). Hmm.\n\nTimeline: I set start date to Nov 15 via calendar; editor showed start \"Tomorrow\". Then set due date to Tomorrow 9 AM. Now start date row shows \"Due date\". So the start date, which was Nov 15, now shows \"Due date\" because... the app interpreted the start date as relative to due date? Wait, I selected Nov 15 in the calendar, not the \"Due date\" shortcut. But because later I set the due date to Nov 15, the app now displays the start date as \"Due date\" (relative). That means the start date was stored *relative* to the due date from the beginning? Hmm, how would selecting a calendar day store a relative offset? Unless: after I selected Nov 15 via calendar, the app stored absolute Nov 15; then when I set due date to Nov 15, the formatter displays start date == due date as \"Due date\" (the relative label), not because it's stored relative but because relative display compares to due date. So the start date label \"Due date\" is just a display convenience when start == due. Similarly \"Day before due\" when start = due-1. Hmm, but then earlier \"Day before due\" persisted because start was Nov 18 = due Nov 19 minus one day. And \"Tomorrow\" when start = tomorrow. So these labels are computed relative to either today or the due date, whichever is more meaningful. Interesting design: start date label relative to due date (\"Due date\", \"Day before due\", \"Week before due\") — probably the picker shortcuts set these relative offsets, but also any start date equal to due date displays \"Due date\". Hmm, wait, that's a weird display for an absolute start date. But maybe tasks.org stores start dates as \"relative to due date\" only when chosen via those shortcuts; choosing a calendar date stores absolute. But here choosing calendar Nov 15 (start) then later due Nov 15 made it display \"Due date\", which suggests display is dynamic, not stored.\n\nHmm, actually, maybe tasks.org's due-date picker \"Due date\" shortcut for start date sets a relative flag. When I selected the calendar day Nov 15, it was absolute. Then when I set due to Nov 15, the start relative label \"Due date\"... would require comparing, so likely the label is dynamic: start date equals due date → \"Due date\". OK.\n\nThis weird display is not in the changed bullets; probably intended feature.\n\nAnyway, for the group header boundary test, set Read docs due Nov 21: tap due row (4), choose Nov 21 in calendar, OK, Save. With full date OFF, the editor due row should show \"Saturday\" or \"Saturday, November 21\". Let's see."}
