ue date (\"Due date\"), perhaps the chip is suppressed because it's redundant? Hmm, but \"Day before due\" also relative and its chip showed \"Wed\" (resolved) earlier! Recall: when Buy milk start = \"Day before due\" (relative), the list chip showed \"Wed\". So relative start dates do show chips (resolved). Then why doesn't Read docs' \"Due date\" relative start show a chip?\n\nWait, when Buy milk start was \"Day before due\", we were in grouped-by-due-date view and the chip showed \"Wed\". Yes. So relative works. For Read docs with start \"Due date\" (same as due), maybe the chip is suppressed because it equals the due date? In due-date-grouped view, the due date is the group header; the start date equals it, so showing a start chip \"Due date\" would be redundant. Maybe suppressed intentionally. Hmm, maybe the chip would say \"Tmrw\" (same as group header \"Due tomorrow\") — redundant. Does the app suppress start chips when start == due? Possibly.\n\nTo test, make Read docs start = due date - 1 (Day before due) instead of equal, with due tomorrow: then chip should show \"Today\"? We could test. But this is getting deep.\n\nAlternatively, the missing start chip for start == due might be the seeded date-badge bug: \"the badge for each date field is now built independently\" — perhaps when start and due are equal, the app considers the start badge duplicate and hides it? Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, does Read docs have a start date? Maybe the \"Due date\" row in the editor is not a start date value but a placeholder \"Due date\" meaning start date = due date relative? Yes. And maybe the app *doesn't* show a start chip for relative start dates of type \"due date\" by design? But \"Day before due\" showed chip. Hmm.\n\nLet me test explicitly: Set Read docs start = \"Day before due\" (due tomorrow Nov 15 → start Nov 14 = today), keep due tomorrow 9AM, grouping by due date, and see if start chip appears. If it appears, then the missing chip only for \"Due date\" type (start==due) — maybe by design (redundant). If it doesn't appear, then relative start chips are broken in this state... but earlier Buy milk showed \"Wed\" for \"Day before due\" — so it works. Hmm.\n\nWait, actually earlier when Buy milk had \"Day before due\", the chip \"Wed\" appeared at 1:16-ish. Let me re-check that observation: after save, the list showed [3] rowBody contains \"Buy milk\" | \"2 litres\" | \"pending_actions\" | \"Wed\", [6] View contains \"pending_actions\" | \"Wed\" @(229,584). Yes. So relative start chip works. Good.\n\nSo maybe start==due chip suppression is intentional (don't show redundant start chip). Hmm, or maybe there IS a bug: with start date equals due date, the start chip is missing (should show \"Tmrw\"/\"Due date\"). Hmm.\n\nLet me test the \"Due date\" shortcut on Buy milk: set start = Due date (Nov 19) and see if chip appears in due-date-grouped view. If no chip, likely intentional (start==due redundant). If chip appears \"Thu\", then the Read docs missing chip is due to something else (maybe because the relative label \"Due date\" not handled). Let's test quickly.\n\nActually, maybe the chip text for start==due would be \"Thu\" and the app hides it because duplicate with group header? Hmm.\n\nHmm, I need to consider the \"date badges\" bullet: \"the badge for each date field is now built independently of the others.\" What bug would a user notice? Possibly: the start date badge (chip) shows the *due* date instead of the start date, or vice versa when both are present. We tested Buy milk start Nov 16/due Nov 19: chip \"Mon\" (Nov 16) and badge \"Nov 19\" — correct. So no mix-up.\n\nCould the bug be that the *time* portion is shown for the wrong field? E.g., a task with due time and start date: the start chip might show due time instead of date? Our Read docs: start=due date (Nov 15), due time 9AM; chip missing. If chip showed \"9 AM\" that'd be wrong. It's missing.\n\nHmm hmm.\n\nLet me test a clean case: Buy milk due Nov 19; set 