{"type": "thinking", "text": "Confirmed: Buy milk has start date \"Today\" but NO start chip appears in the row (grouping None). Earlier start date Monday 5 PM showed chip \"Mon 5 PM\". So the start date chip is hidden when the start date is today (and maybe past). Is this a bug? The start date chip setting is enabled. The row should show the start date badge \"Today\" like it shows \"Mon 9 AM\". Hmm.\n\nWait: maybe the chip is hidden because the start date has no time and is today? Or because start date today means the task is \"started\" and the chip is intentionally hidden? Let me test start = tomorrow (Nov 15) no time, and start = yesterday (Nov 13), to see the rule.\n\n- Tomorrow: expect chip \"Tmrw\" (we saw earlier Read docs start Tomorrow showed chip \"Tmrw\"). So tomorrow shows.\n- Yesterday: maybe hidden or shows \"Yest\".\n\nIf today is the only hidden case, that's bizarre. Hmm, tasks.org might intentionally hide the start date chip when the task has *started* (start <= today) — actually that makes some sense: once a task has started, showing \"start date\" is less useful? Hmm, I don't recall tasks.org doing that.\n\nWait, maybe the start date chip is only shown when the start date is in the future because the chip means \"this task starts on X\" (i.e., it's hidden/not yet actionable). Once start <= now, the task is active, so the start chip disappears. That's plausible UI logic! Hmm, but the chip setting \"Start date\" suggests it should show.\n\nLet me test start = yesterday (Nov 13) to see if chip appears (\"Yest\") or hidden. If hidden too, then the rule is \"hide start chips for start dates <= today\". That could be intentional. If it shows \"Yest\" but not \"Today\", that'd be odd → bug.\n\nLet's test yesterday: open Buy milk, start picker, tap Nov 13, OK, Save. No time. Check chip.\n\nActually, careful: start date > due date? Due Thursday Nov 19, start Nov 13 fine.\n\nLet me do it."}
